        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "50172ad1-3f59-50e5-ad79-9fae7eb2e117",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Kim Dong-wook, Kim Yong-hwa, Sung Dong-il\n- Works: Coffee Prince, Take Off, Happy Killers, Romantic Heaven, The Concubine, Foxy Festival, Children..., The Suicide Forecast, The Client\n- Topics: South Korean film, television series, acting careers, box office\n\nFacts:\n- In 2007, Kim Dong-wook became a star through a supporting role in the popular TV series \"Coffee Prince\" after earlier appearances in student short films and minor parts.\n- In 2009, \"Take Off\" was released as a South Korean film written and directed by Kim Yong-hwa.\n- In 2009, \"Take Off\" became the second most attended film of the year in South Korea with 8,392,953 admissions.\n- In 2009, Kim Dong-wook followed his \"Coffee Prince\" breakthrough with the box office hit \"Take Off.\"\n- Subsequently, Sung Dong-il became known as one of Korean cinema's most reliable supporting actors and appeared in films including \"Take Off,\" \"Foxy Festival,\" \"Children...,\" \"The Suicide Forecast,\" and \"The Client.\"\n- In 2010, Kim Dong-wook starred in \"Happy Killers.\"\n- In 2011, Kim Dong-wook starred in \"Romantic Heaven.\"\n- In 2012, Kim Dong-wook received the best reviews of his career for playing an obsessed and tormented king in the period drama \"The Concubine.\"",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "3435c502-f9a7-52e5-8829-8c619e37c434",
        "text": "Page: Kim Dong-wook. Sentence: After appearing in student short films and several minor parts, Kim became a star through his supporting role in the popular TV series \"Coffee Prince\" (2007), followed by box office hit \"Take Off\" (2009).\nPage: Kim Dong-wook. Sentence: He then starred in \"Happy Killers\" (2010) and \"Romantic Heaven\" (2011), but it was his acclaimed performance as an obsessed and tormented king in 2012 period drama \"The Concubine\" that brought Kim the best reviews of his career yet.\nPage: Take Off (2009 film). Sentence: Take Off (, Hanja:國家代表, literally \"National Representative\" or \"National Athlete\" or \"National Team\") is a 2009 South Korean film written and directed by Kim Yong-hwa.\nPage: Take Off (2009 film). Sentence: The film was the 2nd most attended film of the year in South Korea with 8,392,953 admissions.\nPage: Sung Dong-il. Sentence: Subsequently, he became one of Korean cinema's most reliable supporting actors, displaying his comic skills and easy charm in films such as \"Take Off\", \"Foxy Festival\", \"Children...\", \"The Suicide Forecast\", and \"The Client\".",
